---

!! Syntax

```
ishikawa-beta
    Effect Name
    Category One
        Cause
        Another cause
    Category Two
        Cause
            Sub-cause
        Cause
```

The first non-keyword line is the ''effect'' (the problem statement), drawn as the arrowhead at the right. Top-level indented lines are ''cause categories'' (the main bones). Further indented lines are ''causes'' and ''sub-causes''.

|!Element|!Role in diagram|
|First line after keyword|Effect / problem (arrowhead, rightmost)|
|Top-level indented lines|Cause categories (diagonal branches off spine)|
|Second-level indented lines|Individual causes under each category|
|Third-level indented lines|Sub-causes nested under a cause|

!! Tips

* Cause categories are paired above and below the spine — an even number produces the most balanced layout.
* There is no `classDef` support in `ishikawa-beta`; the diagram is purely structural (no color legend needed).
* The "6M" categories (Machine, Method, Material, Measurement, Man/People, Mother Nature/Environment) are a common starting point for manufacturing and software root-cause analysis.
* `ishikawa-beta` also accepts `ishikawa` without the beta suffix, but using `ishikawa-beta` makes the beta status explicit.

